# Plan: implement `rb_destroy`

## Context

`src/rbtree.c` now has `struct rbtree`, `struct rb_node`, and `rb_create`
(`src/rbtree.c:48`), which makes two allocations per tree: the `struct rbtree`
and a `nil` sentinel node. There is currently no way to release a tree, so every
`rb_create` leaks and `make asan` / `make memcheck` can never pass. `rb_destroy`
is the matching teardown.

Contract (`include/rbtree.h:28`):

```c
/* Frees all nodes, key copies, and (if owned) values. NULL-safe. */
void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t);
```

Scope: **`rb_destroy` only.** No test harness in this change (still no `main` in
`tests/test_rbtree.c`), so verification remains a standalone compile check —
but after this, a `create`/`destroy` smoke test becomes possible as the next
step.

## Design

Ownership, from the field comments in `src/rbtree.c` and the header:
- `node->key` — always a heap copy owned by the tree → `free` it.
- `node->value` — owned iff `t->value_free != NULL` → call `t->value_free(value)`
  in that case, matching how `rb_insert` / `rb_delete` will invoke it.
- the `nil` sentinel — a separate `rb_create` allocation, never carries a real
  key/value → `free` the node itself, nothing else.
- the `struct rbtree` — `free` last.

Traversal: **recursive post-order** via a `static` helper. A red-black tree's
height is bounded by `2·log2(n+1)`, so recursion depth is tiny even for the
fuzz sizes (n = 1e5 → depth < 34); no explicit stack, no VLA, no allocation
during teardown.

### Code to add to `src/rbtree.c` (after `rb_create`)

```c
/* Post-order free of one subtree. Depth is O(log n) for a valid RB tree. */
static void rb_free_subtree(rbtree_t *t, rb_node_t *n) {
    if (n == t->nil) {
        return;
    }
    rb_free_subtree(t, n->left);
    rb_free_subtree(t, n->right);
    free(n->key);
    if (t->value_free != NULL) {
        t->value_free(n->value);
    }
    free(n);
}

void rb_destroy(rbtree_t *t) {
    if (t == NULL) {
        return; /* NULL-safe per contract */
    }
    rb_free_subtree(t, t->root);
    free(t->nil);
    free(t);
}
```

Notes:
- `if (t == NULL)` guard satisfies "NULL-safe".
- The helper is `static` (internal linkage) — not part of the graded contract.
- No reset of `t->root` / `t->size`: the struct is freed immediately after.
- `value` is passed to `value_free` without a NULL check, deliberately matching
  the intended `rb_insert` / `rb_delete` call sites; keep all three consistent.

## Files

- `src/rbtree.c` — add `rb_free_subtree` (static helper) + `rb_destroy`.

## Verification

- Compile-check with the project's exact flags:
  `gcc -std=c23 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -g -O1 -Iinclude -c src/rbtree.c -o /dev/null`
  — no warnings, no errors.
- Full `make test` / `asan` / `memcheck` still cannot run (no `main` in
  `tests/test_rbtree.c`). Next step to make them meaningful: add a minimal
  `main` that does `rb_destroy(rb_create(NULL));` and `rb_destroy(NULL);` — that
  pair should then be clean under asan and valgrind.
- No commit from this state (not green per the CLAUDE.md rule).

